WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:21.180
Time is a concept that's fascinated
philosophers and thinkers throughout

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:27.900
history. The 5th century Greek
philosopher Heraclitus said time is a

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.590
game played beautifully by children.
Albert Einstein said time is an illusion.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:39.000
We've never really understood what it
was and least of all understood what it

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.900
is in complex systems. This project seeks to develop models and tools and

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:52.949
frameworks to address the many faces of time in the worlds that we live in. So

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:57.720
the arrow of time traditionally refers
to the increase of entropy or disorder

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:05.940
in a closed system. The adaptive arrow of time relates to the increase in order

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:12.540
that's functional in an open system. In
complex systems there are multiple

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:18.570
arrows of time: there's an arrow of time
in your cell, there's an arrow of time in

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:24.450
a kidney, there's an arrow of time in an
ecosystem, and those are the complex

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:30.090
arrows of time. They integrate over many
adaptive arrows of time. The adaptive

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:37.109
arrow of time relies on entropy because
the increase in order in a complex

00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:42.960
system has to correspond to an increase
of disorder in the world. And so this

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.890
project seeks to bring all of those
arrows together and we call that "complex

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:54.060
time." One of the foundational differences
between complex systems and simple

00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:01.310
physical systems in relation to time is
that complex systems can perceive time

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:07.740
whereas a solar system for example, or a
planet, exists within time and we explain

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.540
processes for example of radioactive
decay as unfolding in time. There's no

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:18.680
sense in which that system encodes time
and uses it adaptively.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:27.820
An issue that everyone cares about is
whether failure of a system is gradual

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:36.640
or catastrophic. It's a given that we're
going to die but it's not clear how. Can

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.690
we maintain function almost impeccably
and perfectly for a long period of time

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:46.810
and then collapse quickly, or do we have
to degrade painfully and slowly? We have

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:51.190
no theory for that, and this is an area
that we are actively working on in

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:56.170
relation to brain function. Could you
maintain almost perfect brain function

00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:02.530
up until the end, or is senescence
inevitable? Can we control how long we

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:12.370
live? Could we predict and intervene to
ensure that a company would survive many

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:17.380
more years? Do we know when our
civilization is going to come to an end?

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:22.450
Are there early warning signs? And if we
can detect them are there mechanisms

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:28.500
that we could deploy to change the
trajectory of the system?

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:35.640
One obvious difference between the aging of technology and aging in biology is

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:42.570
that technology tends to accumulate more error, whereas biology tends to eliminate

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:48.720
error through death and extinction. So
when you consider software, every time

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:54.810
there's a bug you don't throw away all
the software. You add a patch. And so an

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:58.770
interesting question is, does software
and technology age in a fundamentally

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:03.990
different way from biological species
and biological organisms? Because you

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:07.590
can't afford to throw everything away.
Because you have to work with what you

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:14.300
have and fix it, and that leads to
extraordinarily messy and kludgy systems

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:19.200
which makes technology and society look a little different from the biological

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:25.200
world. Another important difference in
the aging of society and organisms is

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.430
that societies are made up of many
organisms all contributing ideas, all

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:36.630
contributing technologies, and so we have to have a collective theory of aging, not

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:40.380
just an individual theory, and that's
something that we certainly don't yet

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:50.120
have. One very intriguing similarity
between for example the power grid and

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:57.630
the nervous system is that catastrophic
failure in both cases can be explained

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:04.560
by contagion across networks, and so in
in both of those cases –&nbsp;in one case you

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.190
have systems of electrical connections
and the other ones axons and synapses –

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:15.450
damage propagates across the neural net or the power grid, taking down individual

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:20.790
components in a very statistically
regular way, so that when you look at

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:24.750
these two systems through the
appropriate mathematical lens they look

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.080
very similar.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:35.379
We're very interested in partnering with
individuals or labs or institutions who

00:05:35.379 --> 00:05:42.610
have highly resolved temporal data that
contain some signature of the arrows of

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:47.620
time. We're looking for economists,
archeologists, anthropologists, computer

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:51.759
scientists, mathematicians, physicists, and so on all who can come together and

00:05:51.759 --> 00:05:56.650
explore the way time plays out in a
variety of different complex systems.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:03.190
Because we have all of these different
communities working together, we can

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:09.330
begin to ask how time in one system
relates to time in another, for example

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:18.159
what is the relationship between sleep
and susceptibility to disease? What's the

00:06:18.159 --> 00:06:25.080
relationship between brain failure and
sleep? What's the relationship between

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:30.490
the perception of time and the structure
of our society? So there are all these

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:34.990
much larger questions that we can begin
to address that could not be addressed

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:38.620
in a disciplinary fashion, as a
consequence of having all of these

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:44.139
communities working together to discover the core patterns in their data and the

00:06:44.139 --> 00:06:49.409
common tools that they can employ. In
some sense what we need is to extend

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:55.270
statistical mechanics to include
heterogeneity and adaptive processes. And

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:59.949
this is a huge challenge because the
tendency has been to use the tools that

00:06:59.949 --> 00:07:06.370
we have on problems where they are not a very good fit. And so a big part of our

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:10.870
initiative has been to bring together
theorists who are willing to explore the

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:15.279
full richness of the data and develop
new tools and frameworks that are

00:07:15.279 --> 00:07:22.780
adequate to explain the regularities
that we observe.

